\section{Introduction}
The rapid advancement of Large Language Models (LLMs) has created a paradigm shift in software engineering (SE), with models now capable of generating code, fixing bugs, and even designing entire software components \cite{hou2023large}. This transformative potential has led to a surge of interest from both academia and industry. To measure the progress of these models, the community has developed a variety of benchmarks, such as SWE-Bench \cite{jimenez2024swebench}, LiveCodeBench (LCB) \cite{zhang2024livecodebench}, and the Aider Polyglot benchmark \cite{goth2024polyglot}.

However, the current practices for executing and reporting on these benchmarks are fundamentally broken, leading to a crisis in reproducibility, transparency, and trustworthiness \cite{li2023reproducibility}. Benchmark results are influenced by a vast set of parameters—from the LLM's training data and inference settings to the precise version of the evaluation scaffold—that are often poorly documented. This makes it nearly impossible to reproduce published findings, turning what should be a scientific process into a frustrating exercise in guesswork.

From an industrial perspective, this is a critical business obstacle. As a company investing heavily in training our own LLMs, we rely on public benchmarks to establish a performance baseline. Without stable and reproducible comparisons, we cannot gauge whether our models are improving or if observed differences are simply artifacts of a divergent evaluation setup. This uncertainty stifles innovation and complicates strategic decisions.

In this paper, we distill our team's practical experience in navigating this chaotic landscape into a set of concrete recommendations for the community. We begin with a motivating example, using our own experimental data to show how seemingly minor variations in parameters can lead to significant swings in benchmark scores. We then present a series of actionable recommendations aimed at improving the transparency and trustworthiness of benchmark reporting. These include practices like requiring cryptographic hashes of model weights for submissions to public leaderboards to ensure verifiable integrity. Our goal is to move the community towards a more robust, reliable, and ultimately more scientific foundation for evaluating LLMs in software engineering.